liA"s's film is studied as a 1902 French work, with the setting told. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | Level 1 | Who gets shown, and how. *How do media makers' choices give or take power, and what does that mean for the people in the frame?* | 30 | ma-g9-read; ma-g9-society | MA:Re7.1.Ia; MA:Re7.1.Ib; MA:Cn11.1.Ia; MA:Cn11.1.Ib | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); producing practiced in the made piece. Works: photographs from Edward S. Curtis, The North American Indian (United States, 1907 to 1930, public domain; the Library of Congress and Northwestern University's digital edition), made for a survey that sometimes staged its subjects; Cara Romero (Chemehuevi, born 1977; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), TV Indians (2017), a staged color photograph answering how screens have shown Native people, described and linked to the artist's own page; SebastiA�o Salgado (Brazil; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), photographs of the Serra Pelada gold mine (Brazil, 1986), described and linked, on labor and who looks at whom | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's shot set and the look-describe-interpret steps. MA:Re7.1.Ia: analyze how parts (frame, light, pose, caption), style and preferences show up in the pieces and in the makers' choices (what Curtis left out of his frames; what Romero put in). MA:Re7.1.Ib: analyze how a piece steers what an audience experiences through more than one sense, on a public domain film clip played with sound on and with sound off, and on a photograph sequence with and without its captions. MA:Cn11.1.Ia: explain and show how media ties to power, equality, social trends and identity, in a made six-image sequence with captions (photographed, drawn or cut from public domain images) titled "Who gets shown?", about a place or group the student chooses, an invented community or a public subject marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.Ib: evaluate the legal (consent, copyright, license), technical and career sides of media work, digital identity included, on Curio Class case cards: an invented young photographer's 