| 3 | Level 1 | Moon shots: ideas and a crew. *How do media makers find an idea and a way to make it, and what does a risk buy them?* | 34 | ma-g9-ideas; ma-g9-craft | MA:Cr1.1.I; MA:Cr2.1.I; MA:Pr5.1.Ia; MA:Pr5.1.Ib; MA:Pr5.1.Ic | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Georges MAcliA"s, A Trip to the Moon (1902) from unit 1, revisited for its tricks (stop and substitute, painted sets); William Kentridge (South Africa, born 1955; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Journey to the Moon (2003), made in his studio from charcoal, a coffee pot and a camera, described and linked to a museum's or the artist's page; Norman McLaren and Evelyn Lambart, Begone Dull Care (Canada, 1949, the National Film Board of Canada, free on its own site, linked, never embedded), painted and scratched straight onto film | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's storyboard and workflow sheet and the role cards. MA:Cr1.1.I: use two named idea methods (a "what if" list and a forced pairing of two unrelated things) to form many ideas, set a goal and solve a problem, in a planning portfolio. MA:Cr2.1.I: write a short list of three to five aesthetic standards for the piece and use them to refine the plan and a prototype around a named inspiration (one of the works), the goal and the setting where it will be shown. MA:Pr5.1.Ia: choose a production role in a team of five and carry it out, logging growth in one art, design, technical or people skill with evidence. MA:Pr5.1.Ib: take a creative risk on purpose to meet a set constraint (one location, no spoken words, twelve shots at most), logged with what it cost and gained. MA:Pr5.1.Ic: combine a usual and an innovative tool, method or material to carry the intent (erase-and-redraw charcoal photographed frame by frame, cutouts on a lit sheet, marks scratched or drawn on a paper strip). The piece: a 60- to 90-second team "moon shot" film, or its paper form, a drawn strip screened through the frame-sequence viewer or as a flip book. Words: idea method, goal, aesthetic standard, prototype, risk, constraint. Showing: the moon shots screened for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.I, a guide look-for on the planning portfolio, scored at the mid-unit checkpoint: two named methods used, many ideas, a goal set and a problem solved; MA:Cr2.1.I, a guide look-for on the portfolio and the two prototypes: the aesthetic standards written and the second prototype refined against them, with inspiration, goal and setting named; MA:Pr5.1.Ia, the kept production log: the chosen role carried out and one skill's growth shown with evidence; MA:Pr5.1.Ib, a guide look-for on the finished piece with its log entry: the risk taken and the constraint met; MA:Pr5.1.Ic, a tool-use task at the teacher's table on the check day (each student shows the combined tool or method on one frame and says the intent it carries), with the piece's look-for. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: managing a whole production and interactivity (Level 2). Tools: the idea-method cards, the aesthetic standards sheet, role cards and the production log, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed strip. Free path: charcoal or soft pencil, paper, a lamp or window light. Short path: 17 days | After unit 2 because its questions about who is shown feed the ideas, and the media-ethics check is in place before a team captures anything. Before unit 4 because a piece refined to the student's own goals needs a prototype and the habit of a log first. The longest unit before mid-year: five ids and the level's first team production | Candidate: a Latin American or Asian maker of a hand-made animation, described and linked, for range | Hazards and controls: charcoal dust (damp wipes, no blowing dust, hands washed); lamps low-power, kept off paper; cords taped; shared devices wiped; card cut by an adult. Kentridge's films often concern apartheid; this film is chosen for its studio process, and the sensitivity agent reads the holder's page and the artist's published words before the setting is told. Team work: a student who cannot work in a team holds every role in turn on a solo piece. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
