## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Level 1 mid-year (week 16, days 77 and 78) | 3 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 2 and 3 in a sitting: an unseen public domain photograph sequence analyzed for its maker's choices (MA:Re7.1.Ia, unit 2) and a short planning task from a new brief using two named idea methods and a set goal (MA:Cr1.1.I, unit 3). Unit 3's production log and finished piece (its end check, day 75) are carried unscored. Respond on day 78. |
| Level 1 year-end (week 32, days 157 to 160) | 5 | 2 | 2 | Samples units 2 to 4: day 157, a sitting on an unseen public domain clip, how it steers experience through sight and sound (MA:Re7.1.Ib, unit 2) and how it is meant and was received, with a reception sheet (MA:Re8.1.I, unit 4); day 158, a media-ethics case card evaluated (MA:Cn11.1.Ib, unit 2) and a short critique of a given rough cut against named criteria (MA:Re9.1.I, unit 4). Unit 5 is assessed by its own two screenings, whose records are carried unscored. Respond on days 159 and 160, with the level's portfolio reflection. |
| Level 2 mid-year (week 16, days 237 and 238) | 8 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 7 and 8 in a sitting: three unseen public domain posters compared for how their parts relate and persuade (MA:Re7.1.IIa, unit 7) and a planning task under new resource limits with methods chosen and a reason given (MA:Cr1.1.II, MA:Cr2.1.II, unit 8). Unit 8's production log (day 235) is carried unscored. Respond on day 238. |
| Level 2 year-end (week 32, days 317 to 320) | 10 | 2 | 2 | Samples units 7 to 9: day 317, a sitting on an unseen public domain clip, persuasion across senses (MA:Re7.1.IIb, unit 7) and purpose and influence in two settings (MA:Re8.1.II, unit 9); day 318, a case card routed through its legal, systemic and career sides (MA:Cn11.1.IIb, unit 7) and a defended evaluation of a given piece answering a printed objection (MA:Re9.1.II, unit 9). Unit 10 is assessed by its premiere and written day. Respond on days 319 and 320. |
| Level 3 mid-year (week 16, days 397 and 398) | 13 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 12 and 13 in a sitting: three unseen public domain pieces of different kinds synthesized (MA:Re7.1.IIIa, unit 12) and a one-page framework proposal for a new system and limits (MA:Cr2.1.III, unit 13). Unit 13's two versions and log (day 395) are carried unscored. Respond on day 398. |
| Level 3 year-end (week 32, days 477 to 480) | 15 | 2 | 2 | Samples units 12 to 14: day 477, a sitting on an unseen public domain piece, its aims and impact with a bias in its reception weighed (MA:Re8.1.III, unit 14) and how it steers across systems (MA:Re7.1.IIIb, unit 12); day 478, a route map for a given piece through its legal, technical, systemic and career sides (MA:Cn11.1.IIIb, unit 12) and a critique of the student's own unit 14 hybrid rebuilt with a new bias check (MA:Re9.1.III, unit 14). Unit 15 is assessed by its showing and results day. Respond on days 479 and 480. |
| Level 4 mid-year checkpoint (week 16, days 557 and 558) | 17 | 1 | 1 | The series checkpoint: the student presents the proposal, the first pieces and the defended reading to a panel of two classmates and the teacher, scored on the Level 3 guides for MA:Cr2.1.III and MA:Cr3.1.IIIa with a higher bar for independence; unit 17's essay is carried unscored. The respond day is the critique and revision plan. |
| Level 4 year-end (week 32, days 637 to 640) | 19 | 2 | 2 | The defended statement: day 637, the portfolio of the series and a written statement; day 638, a defended talk on the series answering two questions (MA:Re9.1.III and MA:Pr5.1.IIIa on the Level 3 guides, independence bar). Unit 19's showing and review are carried unscored. Respond on days 639 and 640, with the final portfolio reflection. |

Each sitting day: the unseen piece is shown twice and the written items take 30 of the 45 minutes; set-up, the
self-check and collecting fit the rest. Every student's evidence is collected once in a window; no artifact is scored
twice. Days above are band course days (Level 2 starts on day 161, Level 3 on day 321, Level 4 on day 481); within each
level the checks fall on the level's days 77 and 78, and 157 to 160.

## Cross-subject

No media arts row of Levels 1 to 4 carries a cross_subject lean (data/progressions/arts.csv), so this course waits on
no other course and no other course waits on it. STANDARD.md 6's media arts and computer science line holds: each
unit teaches the one idea of privacy, consent or copyright it needs in a short step (units 1, 2, 7, 12 and 18), and
leans on no computer science course (computer science is not core, lesson 32). Because a level is open to students in
any grade, no contact below assumes a grade's course was taken; each is practiced, not owned, with its "if later"
line. All are core courses.

### English language arts, grades 9 to 12

| Used here | Contact | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Written analysis and critique (units 2, 4, 7, 9, 12, 14, 17) | Argument and analysis writing with claim and evidence (W.9-10.1, W.11-12.1) | The unit's sitting uses a paragraph frame (claim, evidence from the piece, reasoning) taught on its first study day; no essay form is assumed before unit 17, which has its own drafting days |
| Critique talk and the defended talk (every unit; units 9, 17, 19) | Collaborative discussion and presenting (SL.9-10.1, SL.11-12.1, SL.9-10.4, SL.11-12.4) | The critique card and the objection card carry the turn rules and the defended talk's steps; the teacher models them on the first critique day |

### Social studies, grades 9 to 12

| Used here | Contact | If social studies has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Works taught with their time and place (every unit; units 2, 7, 9, 12) | Sourcing and context of primary sources (the high school history courses) | The unit's work card gives the setting (when, where, by whom, for what) and the sourcing questions itself (bar criterion 7) |

### Mathematics, grades 9 to 12

Unit 12 reads Du Bois's 1900 charts and unit 15 compares audience results on a before-and-after card: counts,
fractions and percent only, taught in earlier grades; no statistics beyond reading a chart and comparing two counts
(lesson 13).

## For the lead

1. **Bridge and review units with an empty Skills cell.** STANDARD.md 3.2 makes the Level 1 bridge and the Level 2
   and 3 reviews units of their own (units 1, 6 and 11 here), which own no row; their Skills cell is empty and their
   Owned standards cell says None, which `frame.py check` reads. Pick: frame-write's after-core section adds one line,
   "a bridge or review unit that owns no row leaves Skills empty and says None in Owned standards", so the other four
   arts 9 to 12 frames do the same.
2. **Row change (one, below).** ma-g9-ideas's note says the bridge is "the first unit"; it is unit 1, before the
   row's own unit 3.
3. **After-core check weeks.** Checks sit in week 16 (mid-year) and week 32 (finals) in every level. Grades 9 to 12
   have no stagger rule but finals weeks, so nothing clashes by rule; the five arts 9 to 12 courses will likely all
   pick weeks 16 and 32, which a student taking two arts levels at once meets in one week. Pick: leave for the lead's
   after-core calendar pass; week 16 is fine for a full-year course.
4. **Level 4 in the calendar.** The band's 640 days spread over four school years in frames.csv, though Level 4 is
   optional and a level is not a grade. Pick: keep, since frame.py needs one year per level, and the course page says
   a school may offer Levels 1 to 3 only (480 days).
5. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional costs a class of 30: $90 (Level 1), $80 (Level 2), $80 (Level
   3), $60 (Level 4), all optional beside a free path, and no device bought. Pick: a ceiling of $100 a class a level
   for high school media arts, with every unit's free path kept.
6. **Facts to confirm at the unit plan.** Dates, holders and whether each maker is living are from memory and are
   checked on the holder's own page when the unit plan is written (lesson 23); none was fetched for this frame. The
   films linked to the National Film Board of Canada's site are free to stream there, not openly licensed, so they
   are linked, never embedded or used as unseen check items.
7. **National time figure.** STANDARD.md 3.4's media arts row is pending for K to 8; for 9 to 12 the 120-hour level
   descriptor sets the dose, so this frame states no weekly figure. The visual and media arts association's high
   school opportunity-to-learn checklist, if one exists, would let the course page name a figure: a download for the
   lead to approve (STANDARD.md 3.4 allows a downloaded file, Desk q-fix-downloads-1).
