 303: | 1 | Level 1 | The bridge: frames, shots and a crew. *What does a media maker need to know before the first real piece?* | 12 | | None | Bridge unit, readiness only (STANDARD.md 3.2): creating, producing and responding at the grade 8 step. Works: Georges MAcliA"s, A Trip to the Moon (France, 1902, public domain; the Library of Congress holds a copy), studied for shots, sequence and the crew behind early film | Readiness check in the last ten minutes of day 1, after the hook: shot names, a three-panel storyboard, the critique card's three steps, two production roles. Then, as readiness and claiming no grade 8 id: the shot set (wide, medium, close, high and low angle) with a card viewfinder; a six-shot sequence planned on the storyboard and workflow sheet and captured by drawing or a shared device; the production role cards; the revision log; the critique card (describe, ask, suggest); the media-ethics check card and the source log; the screening-plan card. Words: shot, angle, sequence, workflow, role, credit. Showing: the six-shot sequences screened in class, unscored. Evidence: the readiness check and a bridge record (each card used once), unscored, so the bridge's days count as days outside the level's standards (12 of 160, 7.5 percent, within the bar's 10 percent). Tools: step cards, the shot-call card set, all eight model cards, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed strip. Free path: card viewfinders, paper, pencils. Short path: 6 days | First because Level 1 assumes no earlier media arts learning and every later unit uses the eight models (STANDARD.md 3.2: Level 1 opens with a bridge of at most 12 days). Short because it teaches only the grade 8 tools Level 1 needs, at their first step | Candidate: whether the readiness check needs a picture-only form for a student new to English | Hazards and controls: shared devices wiped and handed back by the device card; cords taped down; card cut by an adult; no filming of anyone without their yes. MAcliA"s's film is studied as a 1902 French work, with the setting told. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 304: | 2 | Level 1 | Who gets shown, and how. *How do media makers' choices give or take power, and what does that mean for the people in the frame?* | 30 | ma-g9-read; ma-g9-society | MA:Re7.1.Ia; MA:Re7.1.Ib; MA:Cn11.1.Ia; MA:Cn11.1.Ib | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); producing practiced in the made piece. Works: photographs from Edward S. Curtis, The North American Indian (United States, 1907 to 1930, public domain; the Library of Congress and Northwestern University's digital edition), made for a survey that sometimes staged its subjects; Cara Romero (Chemehuevi, born 1977; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), TV Indians (2017), a staged color photograph answering how screens have shown Native people, described and linked to the artist's own page; SebastiA�o Salgado (Brazil; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), photographs of the Serra Pelada gold mine (Brazil, 1986), described and linked, on labor and who looks at whom | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's shot set and the look-describe-interpret steps. MA:Re7.1.Ia: analyze how parts (frame, light, pose, caption), style and preferences show up in the pieces and in the makers' choices (what Curtis left out of his frames; what Romero put in). MA:Re7.1.Ib: analyze how a piece steers what an audience experiences through more than one sense, on a public domain film clip played with sound on and with sound off, and on a photograph sequence with and without its captions. MA:Cn11.1.Ia: explain and show how media ties to power, equality, social trends and identity, in a made six-image sequence with captions (photographed, drawn or cut from public domain images) titled "Who gets shown?", about a place or group the student chooses, an invented community or a public subject marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.Ib: evaluate the legal (consent, copyright, license), technical and career sides of media work, digital identity included, on Curio Class case cards: an invented young photographer's profile, a release form, a license, a job posting. Words: frame, caption, gaze, representation, consent, digital identity. Showing: the "Who gets shown?" sequences on the class wall or screen. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.Ia, a mid-unit sitting item (after the study days): parts, style and the maker's choices in an unseen public domain photograph sequence; MA:Re7.1.Ib, a sitting item on the end check day: how an unseen public domain clip steers experience through sight and sound, played twice; MA:Cn11.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the kept sequence: a link between media and power, equality, a trend or identity, shown in the images and named in the captions; MA:Cn11.1.Ib, the kept media-ethics check on the student's own sequence plus a written evaluation of one case card's legal, technical and career sides, digital identity included. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: propaganda and commerce (Level 2). Tools: work cards, case cards, the media-ethics check card, Curio Class's own; a work viewer or printed images. Free path: public domain images printed, paper, scissors, tape. Short path: 15 days | After the bridge because analysis uses its shot words and look-describe-interpret steps; first of the level's paired units because reading media and its power comes before making for an audience, and unit 3's ideas start from the questions raised here. The media-ethics check grows here to digital identity before any team production in unit 3 | Candidate: a Curio Class original clip as the unseen work, so its rights are certain | Native representation: the sensitivity agent first reads the Chemehuevi Indian Tribe's own pages, the artist's own statement on TV Indians and the Library of Congress pages on Curtis; the unit picks Curtis plates that do not show a ceremony, says plainly that some of his images were staged and that some show ceremonial dress, studies them by looking only, and no student restages or imitates them (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line). Salgado's mine photographs show hard labor: the agent reads the holder's page; no student is asked about their own family's work. Identity tasks offer an invented or public option marked the same; no student's own profile or account is used. Hazards and controls: shared devices wiped; scissors; no filming of anyone without their yes. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 305: | 3 | Level 1 | Moon shots: ideas and a crew. *How do media makers find an idea and a way to make it, and what does a risk buy them?* | 34 | ma-g9-ideas; ma-g9-craft | MA:Cr1.1.I; MA:Cr2.1.I; MA:Pr5.1.Ia; MA:Pr5.1.Ib; MA:Pr5.1.Ic | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Georges MAcliA"s, A Trip to the Moon (1902) from unit 1, revisited for its tricks (stop and substitute, painted sets); William Kentridge (South Africa, born 1955; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Journey to the Moon (2003), made in his studio from charcoal, a coffee pot and a camera, described and linked to a museum's or the artist's page; Norman McLaren and Evelyn Lambart, Begone Dull Care (Canada, 1949, the National Film Board of Canada, free on its own site, linked, never embedded), painted and scratched straight onto film | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's storyboard and workflow sheet and the role cards. MA:Cr1.1.I: use two named idea methods (a "what if" list and a forced pairing of two unrelated things) to form many ideas, set a goal and solve a problem, in a planning portfolio. MA:Cr2.1.I: write a short list of three to five aesthetic standards for the piece and use them to refine the plan and a prototype around a named inspiration (one of the works), the goal and the setting where it will be shown. MA:Pr5.1.Ia: choose a production role in a team of five and carry it out, logging growth in one art, design, technical or people skill with evidence. MA:Pr5.1.Ib: take a creative risk on purpose to meet a set constraint (one location, no spoken words, twelve shots at most), logged with what it cost and gained. MA:Pr5.1.Ic: combine a usual and an innovative tool, method or material to carry the intent (erase-and-redraw charcoal photographed frame by frame, cutouts on a lit sheet, marks scratched or drawn on a paper strip). The piece: a 60- to 90-second team "moon shot" film, or its paper form, a drawn strip screened through the frame-sequence viewer or as a flip book. Words: idea method, goal, aesthetic standard, prototype, risk, constraint. Showing: the moon shots screened for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.I, a guide look-for on the planning portfolio, scored at the mid-unit checkpoint: two named methods used, many ideas, a goal set and a problem solved; MA:Cr2.1.I, a guide look-for on the portfolio and the two prototypes: the aesthetic standards written and the second prototype refined against them, with inspiration, goal and setting named; MA:Pr5.1.Ia, the kept production log: the chosen role carried out and one skill's growth shown with evidence; MA:Pr5.1.Ib, a guide look-for on the finished piece with its log entry: the risk taken and the constraint met; MA:Pr5.1.Ic, a tool-use task at the teacher's table on the check day (each student shows the combined tool or method on one frame and says the intent it carries), with the piece's look-for. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: managing a whole production and interactivity (Level 2). Tools: the idea-method cards, the aesthetic standards sheet, role cards and the production log, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or the printed strip. Free path: charcoal or soft pencil, paper, a lamp or window light. Short path: 17 days | After unit 2 because its questions about who is shown feed the ideas, and the media-ethics check is in place before a team captures anything. Before unit 4 because a piece refined to the student's own goals needs a prototype and the habit of a log first. The longest unit before mid-year: five ids and the level's first team production | Candidate: a Latin American or Asian maker of a hand-made animation, described and linked, for range | Hazards and controls: charcoal dust (damp wipes, no blowing dust, hands washed); lamps low-power, kept off paper; cords taped; shared devices wiped; card cut by an adult. Kentridge's films often concern apartheid; this film is chosen for its studio process, and the sensitivity agent reads the holder's page and the artist's published words before the setting is told. Team work: a student who cannot work in a team holds every role in turn on a solo piece. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 306: | 4 | Level 1 | Tone and emphasis. *How does a maker make a piece feel the way they mean it to, and how do we judge it while it is still being made?* | 38 | ma-g9-make; ma-g9-judge | MA:Cr3.1.Ia; MA:Cr3.1.Ib; MA:Re8.1.I; MA:Re9.1.I | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Yasujir�? Ozu, Tokyo Story (Japan, 1953), a still, low camera and long pauses that carry a family's distance, one scene described and linked to a film archive's page; Wanuri Kahiu (Kenya, born 1980; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Pumzi (2009), a short film whose color and sound set the tone of a dry future, described and linked; a public domain newsreel from the Library of Congress, studied for how it was received in its own time on a Curio Class reception sheet | Retrieval in day 2's opener: unit 3's aesthetic standards and the revision log. MA:Cr3.1.Ia: make deliberate choices organizing content and style with emphasis and tone (what is big, long, loud, held; what is cut), in a 2-minute image-and-sound piece, a twelve-image photo essay with a sound bed, or a paper strip with a live sound score. MA:Cr3.1.Ib: refine aesthetic quality and accent style elements to match the student's own stated goals, logged change by change. MA:Re8.1.I: analyze how a piece is meant and how it was received, in a personal setting and a cultural setting, using the reception sheet (who saw it, where, what was said). MA:Re9.1.I: evaluate the student's own work at two key production stages (the rough cut and the final) against identified criteria, weighing context and aims. Words: emphasis, tone, pacing, sound bed, reception, criteria. Showing: the pieces in a class screening. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the finished piece: two deliberate choices of emphasis or tone, named in the log; MA:Cr3.1.Ib, the kept revision log: two refinements, each tied to a stated personal goal; MA:Re8.1.I, a sitting item on the end check day: how an unseen public domain piece is meant and how its reception sheet shows it was received, in a personal and a cultural setting; MA:Re9.1.I, two kept written critiques of the student's own piece, the first at the rough cut (the mid-unit assessment) and the second at the final, each against named criteria with context and aims weighed. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: refining for a chosen audience's impact (Level 2). Tools: the revision log, the criteria card, reception sheets, Curio Class's own; a work viewer. Free path: paper strip, found-sound objects for a live score. Short path: 19 days | After mid-year and unit 3 because tone and emphasis are worked on a piece of the student's own, after a team prototype taught the log and standards. Before unit 5 because the collection needs finished pieces, and judging at production stages comes before judging a showing | Candidate: a sound-only work (a public domain radio piece) beside the two films, for the sound sense | Tokyo Story is about aging parents and grief: the unit plan names the scene shown; grief is studied in the film, never asked of a student. Pumzi's setting is a future without water: the sensitivity agent reads the maker's own published words. Personal goals and taste are the student's own; a choice of subject is never marked down. Hazards and controls: headphones low; screen breaks; shared devices wiped. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 307: | 5 | Level 1 | A collection for two audiences. *How can media weave many sources and forms into one showing, and how does media build culture?* | 40 | ma-g9-present; ma-g9-sources | MA:Pr4.1.I; MA:Pr6.1.Ia; MA:Pr6.1.Ib; MA:Cn10.1.Ia; MA:Cn10.1.Ib | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating in the blended piece. Works: the National Museum of the American Indian's Native Knowledge 360A� online teaching pages (Smithsonian, United States, living Native educators and makers), studied as media that builds culture through online learning, linked; public domain films from the Prelinger Archives and the Library of Congress, as remix sources; Kahiu's Pumzi from unit 4, revisited for how a film travels to festivals, schools and screens | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's screening-plan card and source log. MA:Pr4.1.I: blend arts and media forms (image, sound, live voice or music, text, movement) into one unified team piece, planning for audience reaction and interaction (a planned moment the audience answers, votes or joins). MA:Pr6.1.Ia: design how the class collection is presented and distributed for two audiences and formats (a screening for a younger class and an evening for families in the school's space, with a printed program). MA:Pr6.1.Ib: judge the first showing and act on an improvement for the second, weighing gains to the maker and the community. MA:Cn10.1.Ia: find, judge and weave outside sources (public domain or openly licensed, logged with license and credit) with the student's own ideas to shape an original piece. MA:Cn10.1.Ib: explain and show how media widens meaning and builds culture, for example through online learning, from the Native Knowledge 360A� pages and the class's own collection. Words: blend, audience, collection, program, distribute, culture. Showing: the collection twice, to the younger class and to families. End-of-unit evidence (two check days, the two screenings), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.I, a look-for marked at the first screening: forms blended into one piece with the planned audience moment working; MA:Pr6.1.Ia, the kept collection showing plan: order, two formats and two audiences, how each meets the work; MA:Pr6.1.Ib, a kept written review after the first screening naming one improvement, with the second screening's look-for showing it made, and the gains to self and community weighed; MA:Cn10.1.Ia, the kept source log and credits on the piece: outside sources judged and woven with the student's own (scored at the mid-unit checkpoint on the rough cut); MA:Cn10.1.Ib, a written account on the second check day: how the collection and one online teaching page widen meaning and build culture. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: school-subject content and virtual channels (Level 2). Tools: the screening-plan card, the source log, a program template, Curio Class's own. Free path: a sheet or wall for a screen, printed programs, a live sound score. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 1 because the collection is built from the level's pieces and the new team piece, and the showing ends the level before the year-end check in week 32. The source log, kept since the bridge, is weighed here for the first time | Candidate: the second audience moved to a community partner's space where the school allows it, with every yes | Native Knowledge 360A�: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's own pages first and the unit uses them as Native educators present them; no student restages a Native practice. Remix: public domain or openly licensed sources only, named on the piece. Showing outside class needs each yes. Hazards and controls: an evening showing in the school's space with the school's own adults; cords taped; no flashing light; headphones low. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 308: | 6 | Level 2 | Review and placement: back in the edit. *What do we carry from Level 1, and where does each of us start?* | 6 | | None | Review unit, inside the standards (STANDARD.md 3.2): creating, producing and responding at the Level 1 step. Works: one public domain film from the Library of Congress, cut two ways by the teacher, as the review's study piece | Readiness check in the last ten minutes of day 1, after the hook: Level 1's idea methods, emphasis and tone, the roles and the screening plan. Review by reteach of Level 1's techniques (a twelve-shot sequence planned, a sound bed laid, a critique at a stage) and the eight model cards in their Level 1 form. Placement: a student new to the level or the course shows a piece or makes the review's short piece, scored against Level 1's year-end check and guides, and starts Level 2 if it meets them; otherwise the course page names Level 1. Words: review, placement, cut, sound bed, stage, plan. Showing: the review pieces in class, unscored. Evidence: the readiness check and the placement record, unscored for Level 2 ids. Tools: the model cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper strip. Short path: 3 days | First in Level 2 because a level requires the one before or a placement (STANDARD.md 3.2), and the review doubles as that placement. At most 6 days | Candidate: a placement guide written from Level 1's year-end check items | Hazards and controls: shared devices wiped; cords taped. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 309: | 7 | Level 2 | Persuasion and truth. *How does media sell, rally and persuade, and how can it tell the truth about what was hidden?* | 32 | ma-g10-read; ma-g10-society | MA:Re7.1.IIa; MA:Re7.1.IIb; MA:Cn11.1.IIa; MA:Cn11.1.IIb | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); producing practiced in the made piece. Works: World War II posters from the National Archives' online exhibit "Powers of Persuasion" (United States, 1941 to 1945, public domain), selling war bonds and rallying workers; El Lissitzky, Beat the Whites with the Red Wedge (Soviet Russia, 1919 to 1920, public domain), a poster that persuades by shape alone; Patricio GuzmA�n (Chile, born 1941; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Nostalgia for the Light (2010), a documentary joining astronomers and the women who search the desert for the disappeared, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's reading of makers' choices and the media-ethics check. MA:Re7.1.IIa: analyze and synthesize how parts relate across several pieces (the posters side by side), adding classmates' feedback on audience impact from a quick poll. MA:Re7.1.IIb: analyze how varied pieces steer experience, intent and persuasion through more than one sense (the poster's shape and color; the film's sound and silence). MA:Cn11.1.IIa: examine how media ties to commerce, power structures, propaganda and what is true, in a made two-sided piece: a persuasive poster or 30-second spot for an invented product or cause, and its "truth panel" that shows how each choice persuades. MA:Cn11.1.IIb: investigate and ethically navigate the legal, technical, systemic and career sides, audience interactivity included, on Curio Class case cards (an invented agency job, an advertising rule, a comment section, a poll that collects data), with the media-ethics check. Words: persuasion, propaganda, commerce, truth, interactivity, synthesize. Showing: the two-sided pieces in a class gallery with the audience poll. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.IIa, a mid-unit sitting item: how parts relate across three unseen public domain posters, with the class poll's feedback used; MA:Re7.1.IIb, a sitting item on the end check day: how an unseen public domain clip persuades through sight and sound, played twice; MA:Cn11.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the kept two-sided piece: the persuasive piece and its truth panel naming commerce, power, propaganda or truth; MA:Cn11.1.IIb, the kept media-ethics check and a written route through one case card's legal, technical, systemic and career sides, with the audience interaction it raises. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: worldwide purposes and strategic navigation (Level 3). Tools: work cards, case cards, the poll card, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper, markers, cut paper. Short path: 16 days | After the review because analysis and the media-ethics check pick up where Level 1's unit 2 left them; first of the level's paired units because unit 8's interactive piece and unit 10's persuasive piece both need the study of persuasion and audience interaction first | Candidate: a persuasive piece by a living maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked | Hard history and propaganda: some wartime posters used racist caricature; the unit chooses posters without it, says in one plain sentence that such posters were made, and shows none. The Chilean dictatorship's disappeared: the sensitivity agent reads the film's own pages and a Chilean memorial body's published words first; no student is asked about their own family's history. Invented products and causes only; no real brand. Hazards and controls: markers non-toxic; shared devices wiped. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 310: | 8 | Level 2 | Fail, try again: making it interactive. *How does a team keep exploring after a test fails, and how can a viewer steer a piece?* | 38 | ma-g10-ideas; ma-g10-craft | MA:Cr1.1.II; MA:Cr2.1.II; MA:Pr5.1.IIa; MA:Pr5.1.IIb; MA:Pr5.1.IIc | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Te Wei and the Shanghai animation studio, Where Is Mama (China, 1960), the first ink-wash animation, reached after long tests, described and linked to a film archive's page; Leanne Allison and Jeremy Mendes, Bear 71 (Canada, 2012, the National Film Board of Canada, free on its own site), an interactive documentary the viewer steers through a park's trail-camera images, linked, never embedded | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's idea methods and production roles. MA:Cr1.1.II: choose idea methods on purpose (picked from the card set with a reason) to produce ideas, sharpen the goal and make the approach more original. MA:Cr2.1.II: design and refine an original prototype in the student's own stated aesthetic within resource limits (the team's time, one device or none, paper). MA:Pr5.1.IIa: manage and produce: the team's producer role rotates so each student leads one production day, logged. MA:Pr5.1.IIb: keep exploring instead of settling, and learn from at least two failed tests on a hard challenge (an ink or wet-medium test, a branching path that confuses), logged with what each failure taught. MA:Pr5.1.IIc: adapt and blend tools, styles and interactivity toward an expressive goal: a branching piece of three choice points, built on a device or as a branching paper deck the audience steers. Words: strategy, personal aesthetic, limits, test, failure, interactivity. Showing: the branching pieces played by another class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.II, a guide look-for on the planning portfolio at the mid-unit checkpoint: methods chosen with a reason, a sharpened goal, an approach made more original; MA:Cr2.1.II, a guide look-for on the prototype and its revision: the student's aesthetic stated and the limits named and met; MA:Pr5.1.IIa, the kept production log: the producer day led and its plan carried out; MA:Pr5.1.IIb, the log's failure entries: two failed tests and what changed after each; MA:Pr5.1.IIc, a tool-use task at the teacher's table on the check day (each student walks one choice point and says how the tool, style and interaction carry the goal), with the piece's look-for. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: framing the question and systems (Level 3). Tools: idea-method cards, the production log, a branching-piece builder or the printed branching deck, Curio Class's own. Free path: ink or watercolor and paper, index cards for the deck. Short path: 19 days | After unit 7 because the interactive piece uses audience interaction studied there; before mid-year so the managed production is assessed before the level's individual piece. Long because it owns five ids and the level's team production | Candidate: a Native or African interactive or animated work, described and linked, for range | Bear 71 is about a grizzly bear's life and death near people: the unit plan names the paths shown; the sensitivity agent reads the agency's page first. Hazards and controls: ink and wet media (covered tables, water spills wiped, hands washed); shared devices wiped; screen breaks. Team work: a solo version with every role taken in turn. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 311: | 9 | Level 2 | Cut and contrast. *How do continuity and collision between shots make meaning, and how do we defend a judgment of a piece?* | 38 | ma-g10-make; ma-g10-judge | MA:Cr3.1.IIa; MA:Cr3.1.IIb; MA:Re8.1.II; MA:Re9.1.II | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Sergei Eisenstein, Battleship Potemkin (Soviet Union, 1925, public domain in the United States), the Odessa steps sequence, studied for its collision cuts in the parts the unit names; Julie Dash (United States, born 1952; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Daughters of the Dust (1991, on the Library of Congress's National Film Registry since 2004), a film of a Gullah Geechee family on the Sea Islands in 1902, studied for its continuity and its long-held scenes, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's emphasis and tone and the critique at two stages. MA:Cr3.1.IIa: make deliberate choices organizing content and style conventions with continuity (matching action, eye lines, sound across a cut) or juxtaposition (collision of shots), in a 2- to 3-minute piece or a twelve-panel strip with a live score. MA:Cr3.1.IIb: refine the look and technical parts so the piece lands with a chosen audience, named before shooting, logged change by change. MA:Re8.1.II: analyze a piece's purpose, meaning and influence across personal, social, historical and cultural settings (each film's own time and later). MA:Re9.1.II: build a defended evaluation through a systematic critique of a piece and its production steps, answering one objection from a classmate. Words: continuity, juxtaposition, montage, convention, influence, objection. Showing: the pieces screened for a chosen audience (another class). End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the finished piece: continuity or juxtaposition used on purpose, named in the log; MA:Cr3.1.IIb, the kept revision log: two refinements tied to the chosen audience's response at the rough-cut test (the mid-unit checkpoint); MA:Re8.1.II, a sitting item on the end check day: an unseen public domain clip's purpose, meaning and influence in two settings; MA:Re9.1.II, a kept written critique of a classmate's piece or a studied piece and its steps, with criteria, evidence and one objection answered. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: hybridization (Level 3). Tools: the revision log, the objection card, Curio Class's own; a work viewer. Free path: paper strip and a live sound score. Short path: 19 days | After mid-year because the individual piece uses unit 8's prototype habits; before unit 10 because the theme piece needs continuity and a chosen audience first, and the defended evaluation feeds the showing's review | Candidate: a third work using collision cuts by a living maker from Asia or Latin America, described and linked | Potemkin's steps sequence shows soldiers firing on a crowd: the teacher previews; the unit shows only the cuts it names and describes the rest; the film is studied as 1925 Soviet state cinema made to persuade. Daughters of the Dust: the sensitivity agent reads the film's own pages and Gullah Geechee Cultural Heritage Corridor pages first; no student imitates Gullah speech or dress. Hazards and controls: headphones low; screen breaks; shared devices wiped. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
 312: | 10 | Level 2 | A curated channel. *How do we curate a collection under one theme for an audience we cannot see, and what does it say about our culture?* | 40 | ma-g10-present; ma-g10-sources | MA:Pr4.1.II; MA:Pr6.1.IIa; MA:Pr6.1.IIb; MA:Cn10.1.IIa; MA:Cn10.1.IIb | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating in the theme piece. Works: Wangechi Mutu (Kenya, born 1972; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), The End of eating Everything (2013), an animated collage on consumption and the earth, described and linked to a museum's page; Skawennati (Kanien'kehA�:ka of KahnawA�:ke, born 1969; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), TimeTraveller (2008 to 2013), a machinima series made in a virtual world that tells history from a Kanien'kehA�:ka point of view, described and linked to the artist's own page; the Library of Congress's online exhibitions, studied as curated collections on a channel | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's collection plan and source log. MA:Pr4.1.II: merge arts, media and school-subject content (a science, history or math idea of the team's choice) into one piece with a steady theme and style. MA:Pr6.1.IIa: curate and design the class collection for varied contexts, a mass audience and a virtual channel included, as a channel plan (a printed channel page, an order, a release plan, the audience it is for). MA:Pr6.1.IIb: judge the in-school premiere and improve it, weighing effects on the makers, the school and society. MA:Cn10.1.IIa: synthesize inner and outer resources (a written reflection on the student's own view, and research) to strengthen a persuasive piece. MA:Cn10.1.IIb: use media to join ideas into new meaning and to reflect on culture, in the piece and a written account. Words: theme, curate, channel, mass audience, introspection, reflect. Showing: the premiere in the school's space; the channel page on the wall (published only where the school controls the space, by the teacher, with each yes). End-of-unit evidence (two check days: the premiere and the written day), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.II, a look-for marked at the premiere: school-subject content merged with a steady theme and style; MA:Pr6.1.IIa, the kept channel plan: the collection curated for a mass audience and a virtual channel, with order, page and release; MA:Pr6.1.IIb, a kept written review of the premiere naming an improvement made to the channel plan or a piece, weighing effects on self, the school and society; MA:Cn10.1.IIa, the kept source log and reflection, scored at the mid-unit checkpoint: introspection and research both used to strengthen the persuasive piece; MA:Cn10.1.IIb, a written account on the second check day: what new meaning the piece joins and what it says about a culture. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: work that holds across platforms and promotion (Level 3). Tools: the channel-plan sheet, the source log, the screening-plan card, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed channel page, paper strip, a live score. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 2 because the channel is curated from the level's pieces and the new theme piece, before the year-end check in week 32; persuasion from unit 7 and the defended evaluation from unit 9 feed the piece and the review | Candidate: a school-subject partner teacher's content check for the theme pieces | Mutu's work concerns consumption and the body; the unit plan names the parts shown. Skawennati's series tells Kanien'kehA�:ka history: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own pages and KahnawA�:ke's own published pages first; no student makes an avatar of a Native person or imitates a Native practice. Introspection tasks offer a public or invented option marked the same. Hazards and controls: premiere in the school's space; cords taped; no flashing light; headphones low. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
