## The pass rule

**Accepted for now (Cory, 2026-11-11), to be tested on the first finished course.** No course has been read
against it yet, so it may prove too hard or too easy.
It copies the shape of the instruments read: alignment, accuracy and feasibility are a door that stops a review, and
the rest is scored.

- **The door: C1, C2, C3 and C14, each passed in full.** A course that fails one is not read further until fixed.
- **C4 to C13, C15, C16:** each is marked pass, part pass or fail. A finished course has no fail and no more than
  three part passes.
- **Pass:** no "fails if" condition is found in the sample. **Part pass:** a condition is found in one sampled unit
  only, and the fix is local. **Fail:** it is found in two or more units, or is built into the course's design.
- **The sample.** The reviewer reads the whole scope and sequence and coverage table, then three whole units picked by the
  reviewer, never by the builder, one from each third of the year: every lesson, page, tool and check in them. A
  course of four units or fewer is read whole. Below, "three units" means this sample.
- The subject part is read the same way: no criterion failed, and the part pass count above covers core and subject
  criteria together.
- **A small course is read the same way.** SEL and library skills courses may be smaller than other courses by
  the nature of the subject (decided 2026-11-11). They are "held to the whole core at the size its scope and sequence states":
  every criterion from C1 to C16 and every criterion of the subject part still applies, and what is measured is
  the number of units and days the scope and sequence itself sets out, not the size of a math or science course. Being small
  waives no criterion. **Smaller means fewer minutes a week, never fewer units** (Cory 2026-11-15, later.csv L43): each
  SEL and library course runs the whole year at one or two sessions a week, or about one every two weeks in the two library band courses (its frame states which), so every standard
  of its framework is still taught, practised spaced out over the year and checked. This fits how schools staff these
  subjects (a counselor or librarian seeing a class once a week).
- **Beyond the three units,** the reviewer reads: for C1, the whole coverage table, tracing ten standards of the
  reviewer's choice to lesson and check item; for C4, the progression page; for C8 and C9, one cumulative check;
  for C13, the approach page; for C3 and C16, all public copy for the course and the logic model.

## The shared core

Layers are those in docs/ROADMAP.md, "The layers": 1 foundation, 2 standards, 3 approach, 4 progressions, 5 scope and sequence,
6 unit plan and section plan, 7 lessons, tools, print and checks. The rows recur across the instruments read in full (see
the sources table). No count per row is given, because the first draft's "Seen in" column was not reliable.

| # | A reviewer looks for | What shows it in a Curio Class course | Layer | Fails if |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| C1 | **Every standard of the course is named and taught in full,** at the depth its wording asks, with little time on content from outside the course | The scope and sequence's standards table with days; a coverage table pointing each standard to lesson, tool and check item; a named edition loaded in data/standards/ | 2, 5 | Any standard lacks a lesson, practice or a check item. A traced standard is taught below its wording. More than 10 percent of teaching days go to content outside the course's standards (our number, reset per subject once its scope and sequence form is known). The edition is not named or not loaded |
| C2 | **A year plan with reasons:** units in order, why, days per unit, minutes per lesson, a shorter path | The scope and sequence: every teaching day, the reason for the order, the stated year and period length, the short path, gap days | 5 | A teaching day has no plan. The order has no stated reason. No minutes per lesson. No short path. The days do not add up to the stated year |
| C3 | **Nothing wrong, nothing sold, authors plain.** (a) Facts, figures, terms and pictures are correct and current, with sources. (b) No product, brand or provider is promoted; opinion is marked. (c) Who wrote it, and how, is stated | The accuracy read at the Finish check, with the reader named; a source line on each fact page; public copy that names no companies and says how the course was made. Health: see its criterion 7 | 7 | (a) An uncorrected error in a key, a definition or a figure; a dated figure with no date or source. (b) Any promotion. (c) No statement of authorship |
| C4 | **Each idea rests on the last:** what earlier grades taught, how units link, what comes next | The subject's progression page; the scope and sequence's builds-on and comes-back columns; the readiness check or opening retrieval task on each unit plan | 4, 5, 6 | A unit leans on an idea nothing earlier taught and no readiness check or retrieval task catches it. No progression page. A band course does not say what the band before taught |
| C5 | **A unit overview that prepares the teacher:** purpose, background knowledge, the unit's words, how it is assessed | The section plan's fields; the teacher page that teaches the teacher | 6, 7 | The teacher page restates the lessons and adds no background. No word list. Assessment not stated |
| C6 | **Lessons ready to teach:** objective, questions, tasks, materials, time per part, grouping | Daily plans and student pages for every scope and sequence day; the supplies list; the projector path on tool days | 7 | A scope and sequence day has no plan or no student page. A lesson lacks an objective, materials or times. The teacher must write the questions or tasks |
| C7 | **New things are taught, then practised with fading help,** spread over time, and they come back | The section plan field naming who holds the model each day and the day it is withdrawn; the plan for what earlier work returns | 3, 6 | A new skill is first met in independent work. No day is named for withdrawing the model. Nothing from earlier units returns |
| C8 | **An assessment system:** checks before, during and after teaching, each tied to named standards, more than one kind of task, doing where the subject is a doing subject, and students judging and tracking their own work | The unaided check in each tool, readiness checks and end-of-unit assessments on each unit plan (where a unit assumes only rows used the year before or earlier the same year, its opening retrieval task's teacher notes, named in the unit plan, are the before-teaching evidence), cumulative checks on the scope and sequence; a student self-check page per unit | 6, 7 | One of the three sizes is missing. An item names no standard. A doing subject is checked by recall alone. Students never judge or track their own work in a unit |
| C9 | **The teacher can score it and act on it:** keys, rubrics, scored samples, what each wrong answer shows, what to do next | Keys that explain each wrong answer and the reteach; a rubric and sample pieces for every open task; the respond day; the class record sheet | 6, 7 | A key gives answers only. An open task has no rubric, or samples at fewer than two levels. No next step. No record sheet |
| C10 | **Every learner works on the course's content:** help for students behind that keeps the goal, deeper extensions, guidance for disabilities, a reading load that fits the grade with help for students who read below it | The three hooks on every section plan; the reteach per readiness gap; extension on the same standard; the section plan's reading load field | 3, 6, 7 | A support lowers the goal or swaps the content. No extension on the same standard. No guidance for disabilities. Reading load field empty, or pages above grade with no help |
| C11 | **English learners are supported inside the lesson:** the language the lesson asks for, key words in depth, frames, levels where the subject can, and help telling a language gap from a content gap | The English learner hook in every tool and teacher page; Word Wall cards; a line in each key on answers that show a language gap | 6, 7 | Supports are a general list not tied to the lesson. No frames. Nothing helps the teacher tell a language gap from a content gap |
| C12 | **People are shown with care and range:** many voices and places, no stereotypes, no shaming, hard facts told plainly | The fresh-agent sensitivity read (FOUNDATION promise 7); a record per unit of whose work and voices appear | 1, 7 | A stereotype or shaming passes. One group carries all the examples. A hard fact is softened until it is false. No read on record |
| C13 | **The approach is explained, and families are told** | The subject's approach page, with sources and how much of each was read; a family note per unit, in English and Spanish, written when the unit is finished | 3, 7 | No approach page. A research claim rests on a source nobody read. A sampled unit has no family note |
| C14 | **It can be run in an ordinary room:** time, space, supplies, cost and teacher knowledge are stated and modest | The supplies list with cheap stand-ins; the paper path for every tool day; time per lesson; a free site | 1, 5, 7 | The course needs space, kit, time or training most schools lack and gives no stand-in. A cost to the school is not stated. A tool day has no paper path |
| C15 | **Screens serve the learning:** tied to the objective, run on school devices, usable without mouse or colour, private, with a non-screen path | The tool's named job in the section plan; the access check; single-file offline tools that store nothing off the device | 6, 7 | A tool has no named job. It fails keyboard-only or colour-free play. It needs an install, an account or a network. It stores data off the device |
| C16 | **Honest about evidence** | A one-page logic model per course; the plain statement that the course has not been tried with children | 1, 3 | Any claim of effect. No logic model. Research cited that nobody on the project read |

**Where our promises cost us marks, by choice.** Curio Class will not build some things rubrics ask for
(FOUNDATION, section 3): data dashboards (Texas names one as an example), adapting to each student and plugging
into a learning management system (Ohio, narrative items), accessible-format files lodged with a national centre
(Oregon), and professional learning tied to the materials (Ohio, scored). Our answers are the class record sheet,
score codes, open pages, print and teacher pages. Professional learning: none for now (decided 2026-11-11). It is
a future area of growth, not a closed door.

### 2. Arts: visual arts, music, theater, dance, media arts

Twenty courses: five disciplines, four bands.

**Rests on.** Texas fine arts rubric (2025) and California's five categories, read in full by the writer;
Oklahoma's rubric (2024; four gateways, the fourth on state law), read in full by the expert.

1. [I] All four artistic processes are in each course: create; perform, present or produce; respond; connect.
2. [I] Technique is taught directly, in steps, and gets harder.
3. [I] The discipline's words are taught and used in making and responding.
4. [I] Students make, adapt or extend their own work and show it, with some choice in what they make or study.
5. [I] Students are taught to read, judge and respond to other artists' work.
6. [I] Students give and get feedback, and reflect to refine their work.
7. [I] Works and traditions come from many times and cultures, with history and setting taught.
8. [I] Assessment ranges across the year: performances and finished works scored with a guide, self-assessment, and
   work kept over time.
9. [I] Pacing fits real arts schedules (rotations, short weeks). Lessons list equipment and safe use of space.

**Fails if:** prompts to express with no technique taught; no performing or showing; response work without the
discipline's words; one culture's canon; a plan that needs a daily period.

**Unsupported.** No rubric treats the five disciplines apart or says how to choose works. A screen cannot teach a
performing art, so the performing is in the room.

**Standards.** data/standards/ncas-performance-standards.txt (2014; a national revision is under way, no date). The
standards are by grade to grade 8; our courses are bands.

**Scope and sequence states:** which grade's standards fall in which year of the band. **Unit plan fills (field 2):** the
technique, the works studied and the showing. **Confidence:** likely for the instruments, thin for the evidence.

## After-core courses (added 2026-11-25)
The after-core subjects (arts, health, physical education, computer science, career education, SEL, ELD, library)
use this brief with these changes. Their own pages outrank the core lessons above where they differ.

- **Read first, in place of items 2, 6 and 7:** the subject's docs/courses/<subject>/STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md in
  full, docs/agents/lenses/<subject>.md, the subject's row in docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md ("Before a scope and
  sequence in this subject") and its bar part, every DESK-ANSWERS.md entry that names the subject, and the
  subject's stage 1 and progression reviews (docs/reviews/prework-<subject>-*, progression-<subject>-*). The core
  frames the course leans on are read at their Units tables only (docs/courses/<core course>/frame.md).
- **The rows:** the course's `rows` cell in data/plans/course-list.csv selects them (`file:` the progression file,
  `grade:` the grades, `strand:` the strand, `|` between values). `python -X utf8 scripts/frame.py check <slug>`
  checks every one is placed once.
- **Band courses** (one course for several grade years, or the high school arts levels): one frame file for the
  band. The Units table gains a `Year` column right after `#` (`K`, `1`, `2`, or `Level 1` and so on), units are
  numbered through the band (unit 1 of year 2 follows the last unit of year 1), and each year is whole on its own:
  its own units, its own end-of-unit assessments and its own mid-year and year-end course-level checks. The
  **Days.** bullet gives the band's total first, then the days a year ("192: 64 a year for three years").
  `frame.py` spreads the band's days over that many school years and notes the unit days by year. A subject page
  that says "one frame per grade year" is met by this shape: each year's part of the file is that year's frame, and
  the scope and sequence later writes one year at a time.
- **Doses:** use the minutes, sessions a week and days a year the subject's STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md settle; never
  the core 160-day yardstick unless the subject sets it. A smaller course means fewer minutes a week, never fewer
  units (Cory 2026-11-15).
- **Course-level checks:** grades K to 8 after-core checks do not take the core subjects' target weeks; place them
  so they do not fall in a week (K to 5) where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a core check for that grade, and say
  which weeks.
- **The seventh Units column** (lead 2026-11-25, from the pilot fix passes): arts heads it "Processes, anchors and
  works", physical education "Cues and settings", health "Skill steps", SEL "Core link and routines", computer
  science "Practices and concept areas", career education "Practices", ELD "Host unit and key language uses", library
  "Inquiry phases".
- **Owned standards cell:** a unit that owns no code (a carry-only or removable sensitive unit) says `None`; the
  check reads `None` and an empty cell alike.
- **A course-level check right after a unit** is that unit's end-of-unit assessment when it covers the unit, as in
  the core frames; say so in the unit's row and the check's "What it checks" cell. Units not followed by a check end
  in their own end-of-unit assessment.
- **Grades 6 to 12:** the year-end check may sit in week 32, the finals week (CALENDAR.md stagger rules 3 and 4).
- **After-core stagger:** each after-core course counts on its own in `frame.py calendar` (the five arts courses of a
  band are five checks, not one). Writers keep checks off core check weeks; the lead runs one calendar pass across
  the after-core frames for clashes among them.
- **Gates the subject sets:** a subject page that holds building until a condition is met (ELD: C11 proven in the
  core courses) still gets its frame now (Cory 2026-11-25: "please see if you can get them all completed"); the
  frame says in **For the lead** which later stage the condition holds.
