156: - **Leave-out.** A school or family may leave out a unit or a work for its community's reasons (STANDARD.md 5); each
157:   unit stands alone. Units that a course page names: unit 2 (it studies early twentieth-century photographs of Native
158:   people, some showing dress or acts that may carry ceremonial meaning, studied by looking only, beside a Chemehuevi
159:   photographer's answer to them), unit 7 (wartime propaganda posters and a film on the disappeared of the Chilean
160:   dictatorship) and unit 11 (a Ho-Chunk maker's film built from family recordings in the Hocak language). Swapping a
161:   work keeps the unit's ids met; leaving a unit out means the course page and the unit's family note name the ids not
162:   claimed that year.
163: - **The ordinary room.** A class of 30 at tables, paper, pencils, card from cereal boxes, charcoal or soft pencil,
164:   scissors, tape, found objects for sound, a wall or a sheet for a screen; nothing must be bought, and every unit
165:   names a free stand-in (STANDARD.md 3.4, APPROACH "Supplies"). One student at home: the parent is the audience and
166:   the critique partner, every team task has a solo version (one student holds every role, named in turn), and the
167:   showing is to the family. The teacher needs no media arts training: each unit's teacher page says what to look and
168:   listen for, in plain words.
169: - **Supplies and cost.** Provisional cost per unit for a class of 30 (consumables, reusable items such as six
170:   flashlights and six clip lamps counted once, printing of step cards, work cards, case cards, sheets and programs),
171:   all optional beside the free path: Level 1 $10, $15, $25, $15, $25 ($90); Level 2 $5, $15, $25, $15, $20 ($80);
172:   Level 3 $5, $15, $20, $15, $25 ($80); Level 4 $15, $5, $15, $25 ($60). No device is bought. Planning figures, not
173:   quotes; the ceiling is under For the lead.
174: - **Devices.** No device per student is required. Where a school has them, one shared school device per team of
175:   five (a camera, a sound recorder, or a computer with a free editor the school approves) is one way in; one teacher
176:   screen may show a work, a step or a playback. No student account is used, and no student signs up for any service.
177:   The owned codes that name capture, tools, interactivity, platforms or online channels (MA:Pr5.1.Ic, MA:Pr5.1.IIc,
178:   MA:Pr5.1.IIIc, MA:Pr4.1.III, MA:Pr6.1.IIa, MA:Cn11.1.Ib, MA:Cn11.1.IIb, MA:Cn10.1.Ib) are met in full on paper: in
179:   this course a media tool is anything that frames, captures, orders, plays or shows pictures and sound, so a
180:   paper-path student captures by drawing what a card viewfinder frames or by a photographed or traced shadow,
181:   animates by a drawn strip or flip book, scores sound on paper and performs it the same way twice, builds
182:   interactivity as a branching paper piece the audience steers, builds across platforms as one piece made for a wall,
183:   a printed booklet and a live reading, and curates a virtual channel as a printed channel page and release plan (the
184:   ids ask to design and curate for channels, not to publish on one); digital identity and online learning are studied
185:   through Curio Class case cards and printed pages. Shared devices are wiped and handed back by the device card's
186:   steps.
187: - **Paper path.** Every day runs on paper, card, objects and printed cards; any Curio Class tool a unit plan adds (a
188:   frame-sequence viewer that plays drawn frames in order, a shot-list and storyboard builder, a branching-piece
189:   builder, a work viewer) has a printed version that does the same job (the flip book or strip, the storyboard sheet,
190:   the branching card deck) and needs no student account. A tool never judges a finished piece and never generates a
191:   student's work (APPROACH "Tools").
192: - **Reading load.** Case cards and work cards are at most one page at a high school reading level, read in class; a
193:   sitting's written items are short-answer and one paragraph, never a long essay, except the Level 4 criticism unit's
194:   essay, which has its own drafting days. English learners: each unit teaches its six words in depth, with a picture,
195:   a clip or a sound and a frame ("The shot ___ makes the viewer ___ because ___."), a student may show a word in a
196:   made piece instead of saying it, and the check marks what the student made apart from what they wrote (APPROACH
197:   "Supports").
198: - **Safety read.** Every unit is hands-on, so every unit gets the fresh safety agent's read
199:   (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md) against three free guides, named here and opened by the agent: the
200:   federal consumer product safety agency's "Art and Craft Safety Guide" (cpsc.gov), the World Health Organization's
201:   "Make Listening Safe" pages on safe headphone volume (who.int), and the web accessibility guidelines' rule on
202:   flashing, success criterion 2.3.1 "Three Flashes or Below Threshold" (w3.org). Rules for every unit, from APPROACH
203:   "Safe use" ("from memory" until that read checks them): no filming, photographing or recording anyone without
204:   their yes (and the family's under 18); no flashing light in any piece, screening or shadow play, and no strobe, with
205:   any studied work that flashes described, not shown; screen breaks, with no student at a screen more than about 20
206:   minutes at a stretch; headphones, where used, at low volume, and no loud sound in anyone's ear; lamps and
207:   flashlights low-power, never shone in eyes, kept off paper and fabric, no laser pointers; device and lamp cords
208:   taped down or none; card cut with a blade only by an adult; filming only in the school's own spaces or where the
209:   school allows, in pairs, never on roads, roofs, water edges or while anyone drives; supplies labeled non-toxic;
210:   charcoal used with damp wipes and hands washed after. Each unit's last column gives its hazards and controls in the
211:   words days.csv will copy. No student sign-off form.
212: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md),
213:   which covers the works with the rest of the unit; no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units with a
214:   cultural, Native or hard-history setting name the communities and the sources the agent studies first: unit 2
215:   (photographs of Native peoples made for a 1907 to 1930 survey; Chemehuevi photography; Brazilian gold miners), unit
216:   3 (South Africa), unit 4 (postwar Japan; Kenya), unit 5 (the National Museum of the American Indian's own teaching
217:   pages), unit 7 (United States wartime posters; Soviet civil war posters; the Chilean dictatorship's disappeared),
218:   unit 8 (China, 1960), unit 9 (the Soviet Union, 1905 and 1925; the Gullah Geechee people of the Sea Islands), unit
219:   10 (Kenya; Kanien'keh�:ka of Kahnaw�:ke), unit 11 (Ho-Chunk), unit 14 (Iran; Senegal and the migrants of Dakar),
220:   unit 15 (W. E. B. Du Bois and Black Georgians in 1900; Inuit media in Nunavut). Every task that ties media to a
221:   student's own life, identity or values (the Cn10 and Cn11 ids in units 2, 5, 7, 10, 12, 15 and 18) offers an
222:   invented, public-figure or class-shared option marked the same (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). No task asks what a
223:   family watches, owns or posts, or for a student's own accounts, and a student's own media habits are never judged;
224:   digital identity is studied on an invented profile. Hard content in studied works (war, a massacre, a dictatorship's
225:   victims, racist caricature) is described and shown only in the parts the unit names, previewed by the teacher, never
226:   role-played. Each section carries health's steps for "If a student tells you something"
227:   (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md) on its teacher page.
228: - **Logic model (one page in the scope and sequence).** Inputs: one 45-minute lesson a day, step cards, work cards,
229:   case cards, paper media tools, found objects, an optional shared device per team. Activities: technique taught in
230:   steps, works studied with their setting, making in production roles with a critique and a revision, a showing or a
231:   sitting each unit, a screening that ends each level. Outputs: a portfolio of five pieces a level (four in Level 4,
232:   plus the series) with reflections, logs and plans; two marks a unit per student on the main look-for. Outcome
233:   claimed: met marks on the level's 18 ids (Level 4: the eleven re-assessed ids on new work) and growth on the
234:   look-fors. Nothing here has been tried with students, and Curio Class makes no claim that arts study raises any
235:   other result (APPROACH "Sources").
