uide look-for on the prototype and its revision: the student's aesthetic stated and the limits named and met; MA:Pr5.1.IIa, the kept production log: the producer day led and its plan carried out; MA:Pr5.1.IIb, the log's failure entries: two failed tests and what changed after each; MA:Pr5.1.IIc, a tool-use task at the teacher's table on the check day (each student walks one choice point and says how the tool, style and interaction carry the goal), with the piece's look-for. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: framing the question and systems (Level 3). Tools: idea-method cards, the production log, a branching-piece builder or the printed branching deck, Curio Class's own. Free path: ink or watercolor and paper, index cards for the deck. Short path: 19 days | After unit 7 because the interactive piece uses audience interaction studied there; before mid-year so the managed production is assessed before the level's individual piece. Long because it owns five ids and the level's team production | Candidate: a Native or African interactive or animated work, described and linked, for range | Bear 71 is about a grizzly bear's life and death near people: the unit plan names the paths shown; the sensitivity agent reads the agency's page first. Hazards and controls: ink and wet media (covered tables, water spills wiped, hands washed); shared devices wiped; screen breaks. Team work: a solo version with every role taken in turn. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
311: | 9 | Level 2 | Cut and contrast. *How do continuity and collision between shots make meaning, and how do we defend a judgment of a piece?* | 38 | ma-g10-make; ma-g10-judge | MA:Cr3.1.IIa; MA:Cr3.1.IIb; MA:Re8.1.II; MA:Re9.1.II | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Sergei Eisenstein, Battleship Potemkin (Soviet Union, 1925, public domain in the United States), the Odessa steps sequence, studied for its collision cuts in the parts the unit names; Julie Dash (United States, born 1952; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Daughters of the Dust (1991, on the Library of Congress's National Film Registry since 2004), a film of a Gullah Geechee family on the Sea Islands in 1902, studied for its continuity and its long-held scenes, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's emphasis and tone and the critique at two stages. MA:Cr3.1.IIa: make deliberate choices organizing content and style conventions with continuity (matching action, eye lines, sound across a cut) or juxtaposition (collision of shots), in a 2- to 3-minute piece or a twelve-panel strip with a live score. MA:Cr3.1.IIb: refine the look and technical parts so the piece lands with a chosen audience, named before shooting, logged change by change. MA:Re8.1.II: analyze a piece's purpose, meaning and influence across personal, social, historical and cultural settings (each film's own time and later). MA:Re9.1.II: build a defended evaluation through a systematic critique of a piece and its production steps, answering one objection from a classmate. Words: continuity, juxtaposition, montage, convention, influence, objection. Showing: the pieces screened for a chosen audience (another class). End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.IIa, a guide look-for on the finished piece: continuity or juxtaposition used on purpose, named in the log; MA:Cr3.1.IIb, the kept revision log: two refinements tied to the chosen audience's response at the rough-cut test (the mid-unit checkpoint); MA:Re8.1.II, a sitting item on the end check day: an unseen public domain clip's purpose, meaning and influence in two settings; MA:Re9.1.II, a kept written critique of a classmate's piece or a studied piece and its steps, with criteria, evidence and one objection answered. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: hybridization (Level 3). Tools: the revision log, the objection card, Curio Class's own; a work viewer. Free path: paper strip and a live sound score. Short path: 19 days | After mid-year because the individual piece uses unit 8's prototype habits; before unit 10 because the theme piece needs continuity and a chosen audience first, and the defended evaluation feeds the showing's review | Candidate: a third work using collision cuts by a living maker from Asia or Latin America, described and linked | Potemkin's steps sequence shows soldiers firing on a crowd: the teacher previews; the unit shows only the cuts it names and describes the rest; the film is studied as 1925 Soviet state cinema made to persuade. Daughters of the Dust: the sensitivity agent reads the film's own pages and Gullah Geechee Cultural Heritage Corridor pages first; no student imitates Gullah speech or dress. Hazards and controls: headphones low; screen breaks; shared devices wiped. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
312: | 10 | Level 2 | A curated channel. *How do we curate a collection under one theme for an audience we cannot see, and what does it say about our culture?* | 40 | ma-g10-present; ma-g10-sources | MA:Pr4.1.II; MA:Pr6.1.IIa; MA:Pr6.1.IIb; MA:Cn10.1.IIa; MA:Cn10.1.IIb | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating in the theme piece. Works: Wangechi Mutu (Kenya, born 1972; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), The End of eating Everything (2013), an animated collage on consumption and the earth, described and linked to a museum's page; Skawennati (Kanien'keh�:ka of Kahnaw�:ke, born 1969; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), TimeTraveller (2008 to 2013), a machinima series made in a virtual world that tells history from a Kanien'keh�:ka point of view, described and linked to the artist's own page; the Library of Congress's online exhibitions, studied as curated collections on a channel | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's collection plan and source log. MA:Pr4.1.II: merge arts, media and school-subject content (a science, history or math idea of the team's choice) into one piece with a steady theme and style. MA:Pr6.1.IIa: curate and design the class collection for varied contexts, a mass audience and a virtual channel included, as a channel plan (a printed channel page, an order, a release plan, the audience it is for). MA:Pr6.1.IIb: judge the in-school premiere and improve it, weighing effects on the makers, the school and society. MA:Cn10.1.IIa: synthesize inner and outer resources (a written reflection on the student's own view, and research) to strengthen a persuasive piece. MA:Cn10.1.IIb: use media to join ideas into new meaning and to reflect on culture, in the piece and a written account. Words: theme, curate, channel, mass audience, introspection, reflect. Showing: the premiere in the school's space; the channel page on the wall (published only where the school controls the space, by the teacher, with each yes). End-of-unit evidence (two check days: the premiere and the written day), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.II, a look-for marked at the premiere: school-subject content merged with a steady theme and style; MA:Pr6.1.IIa, the kept channel plan: the collection curated for a mass audience and a virtual channel, with order, page and release; MA:Pr6.1.IIb, a kept written review of the premiere naming an improvement made to the channel plan or a piece, weighing effects on self, the school and society; MA:Cn10.1.IIa, the kept source log and reflection, scored at the mid-unit checkpoint: introspection and research both used to strengthen the persuasive piece; MA:Cn10.1.IIb, a written account on the second check day: what new meaning the piece joins and what it says about a culture. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: work that holds across platforms and promotion (Level 3). Tools: the channel-plan sheet, the source log, the screening-plan card, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed channel page, paper strip, a live score. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 2 because the channel is curated from the level's pieces and the new theme piece, before the year-end check in week 32; persuasion from unit 7 and the defended evaluation from unit 9 feed the piece and the review | Candidate: a school-subject partner teacher's content check for the theme pieces | Mutu's work concerns consumption and the body; the unit plan names the parts shown. Skawennati's series tells Kanien'keh�:ka history: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own pages and Kahnaw�:ke's own published pages first; no student makes an avatar of a Native person or imitates a Native practice. Introspection tasks offer a public or invented option marked the same. Hazards and controls: premiere in the school's space; cords taped; no flashing light; headphones low. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
313: | 11 | Level 3 | Review and placement: the systems we work in. *What do we carry from Level 2, and how do media reach the whole world?* | 6 | | None | Review unit, inside the standards (STANDARD.md 3.2): creating, producing and responding at the Level 2 step. Works: Sky Hopinka (Ho-Chunk, born 1984; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Kicking the Clouds (2021), a short film built from his family's recordings of the Hocak language, described and linked to the maker's or distributor's page, studied as the review's piece and as a bridge to unit 12 | Readiness check in the last ten minutes of day 1, after the hook: Level 2's methods and limits, continuity and juxtaposition, the defended evaluation and the channel plan. Review by reteach of Level 2's techniques and the eight model cards in their Level 2 form. Placement as in unit 6, against Level 2's year-end check and guides. Words: system, platform, archive, review, placement, purpose. Showing: the review pieces in class, unscored. Evidence: the readiness check and the placement record, unscored for Level 3 ids. Tools: the model cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper strip. Short path: 3 days | First in Level 3, the review that doubles as placement (STANDARD.md 3.2); its study piece opens unit 12's question of personal and worldwide purpose | Candidate: the placement guide reused from unit 6's form | Hocak language and family recordings: the sensitivity agent reads the maker's own published words and the Ho-Chunk Nation's own pages first; the film is studied by watching and listening only, no student imitates the language or the recordings. Hazards and controls: headphones low; shared devices wiped. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
314: | 12 | Level 3 | Many media, one world. *How do the best media works reach across systems and borders, and what purpose should our work serve?* | 32 | ma-g11-read; ma-g11-society | MA:Re7.1.IIIa; MA:Re7.1.IIIb; MA:Cn11.1.IIIa; MA:Cn11.1.IIIb | Responding (Re7); connecting (Cn11); producing practiced in the made piece. Works: Agn�s Varda, The Gleaners and I (France, 2000), an essay film made on a small camera about people who gather what others leave, described and linked; Hopinka's Kicking the Clouds from unit 11, revisited across the systems of language, archive and festival; W. E. B. Du Bois and his students, the data portraits made for the 1900 Paris Exposition (United States, the Library of Congress, public domain), charts that carried Black Georgians' lives to a world audience | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 2's comparison and persuasion across senses. MA:Re7.1.IIIa: synthesize how parts relate and affect audiences across many kinds of pieces (an essay film, a language film, a set of hand-drawn charts). MA:Re7.1.IIIb: review top-quality examples the student finds and analyze how they steer, persuade and communicate across systems (art world, broadcast, online, school). MA:Cn11.1.IIIa: show how a media piece connects to personal and worldwide purposes, in a made short essay piece (an image, sound and text essay of 90 seconds, or its paper form) on a gathering, a language or a set of facts the student chooses, an invented or public subject marked the same. MA:Cn11.1.IIIb: investigate critically and navigate strategically the legal, technical, systemic and career sides, mapping one route for the student's own piece to a festival, an archive or a school channel. Words: synthesize, system, essay film, purpose, worldwide, strategy. Showing: the essay pieces in class with the route maps. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Re7.1.IIIa, a mid-unit sitting item: how parts relate and affect audiences across three unseen public domain pieces of different kinds; MA:Re7.1.IIIb, a kept review of two top-quality examples the student found (logged in the source log), written on the end check day; MA:Cn11.1.IIIa, a guide look-for on the kept essay piece: a personal and a worldwide purpose both carried; MA:Cn11.1.IIIb, the kept route map with its media-ethics check: legal, technical, systemic and career steps named and chosen with reasons. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the extended defended reading (Level 4). Tools: the route-map sheet, case cards, the media-ethics check, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed public domain charts and images, paper. Short path: 16 days | After the review; first of Level 3's paired units because the student-set problems of units 13 to 15 start from a purpose found here, and the route map sets the system unit 13 works within | Candidate: a top-quality example list Curio Class keeps for students who find none, drawn from public domain and free agency sources | Du Bois's portraits show Black Georgians' lives in 1900, a generation after slavery: the sensitivity agent reads the Library of Congress pages and published scholarship on the portraits first. Varda's film shows poverty: no student is asked about their own family's means. Hazards and controls: shared devices wiped; screen breaks; filming only where the school allows, in pairs. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
315: | 13 | Level 3 | Systems and rules. *How can a set of rules or a system become the idea of a piece, and what question is the team really asking?* | 38 | ma-g11-ideas; ma-g11-craft | MA:Cr1.1.III; MA:Cr2.1.III; MA:Pr5.1.IIIa; MA:Pr5.1.IIIb; MA:Pr5.1.IIIc | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); producing (Pr5); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Vera Moln�r (Hungary and France, 1924 to 2023), (Des)Ordres (1974), plotted squares changed by rule and chance, described and linked to a museum's page; Rafael Lozano-Hemmer (Mexico and Canada, born 1967; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Vectorial Elevation (1999), searchlights over Mexico City's main square designed by people online, described and linked to the artist's own page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 2's strategic methods, limits and interactivity. MA:Cr1.1.III: pair design principles (repetition, variation, rhythm) with idea methods to produce original ideas and a new invention. MA:Cr2.1.III: combine the student's developed aesthetic with knowledge of a system (rules, a platform, a public space) to propose an original framework within complex limits, written as a one-page proposal. MA:Pr5.1.IIIa: use practiced art, design, technical and people skills to manage and produce the team's piece. MA:Pr5.1.IIIb: use creativity and adaptability to frame the question the piece asks and solve a complex challenge, logged. MA:Pr5.1.IIIc: adapt the toolset, styles and systems across a conventional and an experimental version of the same piece. The piece: a rule-based or generative piece (a sequence made by written rules on paper or on a device, or an audience-driven piece in the school's space). Words: design principle, rule, system, framework, experimental, toolset. Showing: both versions shown side by side. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr1.1.III, a guide look-for on the planning portfolio at the mid-unit checkpoint: design principles paired with methods, original ideas; MA:Cr2.1.III, the kept proposal: an aesthetic, a system and complex limits joined in an original framework; MA:Pr5.1.IIIa, the kept production log: the production managed with skills named and shown; MA:Pr5.1.IIIb, a guide look-for on the log and the piece: the framed question and the solved challenge; MA:Pr5.1.IIIc, a tool-use task at the teacher's table on the check day (each student shows how the toolset and style changed between the conventional and experimental versions), with the two versions' look-for. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a self-directed series (Level 4). Tools: rule cards, the proposal sheet, the production log, Curio Class's own; a frame-sequence viewer or paper. Free path: grid paper, rulers, dice for chance rules. Short path: 19 days | After unit 12 because the framework works inside a system the route map named; before mid-year so the team production is assessed before the hybrid piece. Long for five ids and the level's team production | Candidate: an African or Asian maker of rule-based or audience-driven work, described and linked, for range | Hazards and controls: an audience-driven piece in a public part of the school uses no flashing light and no strobe and is cleared with the school; lamps low-power; shared devices wiped. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
316: | 14 | Level 3 | Hybrids. *What happens when a piece joins forms that are not meant to meet, and how do we judge our own bias?* | 38 | ma-g11-make; ma-g11-judge | MA:Cr3.1.IIIa; MA:Cr3.1.IIIb; MA:Re8.1.III; MA:Re9.1.III | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Shirin Neshat (Iran, working in the United States, born 1957; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Rapture (1999), a two-screen video installation of men in a fort and women on a shore, described and linked to a museum's page; Mati Diop (France and Senegal, born 1982; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Atlantics (2019), a film that joins a story of young men leaving Dakar by sea with a ghost story, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 2's continuity and juxtaposition and the defended evaluation. MA:Cr3.1.IIIa: synthesize content and processes into a complex project with compelling meaning, using hybridization (two screens, or film with animation, or data with story). MA:Cr3.1.IIIb: polish every element so the piece reaches its intended viewers and settings, logged. MA:Re8.1.III: analyze the aims, meaning and impact of diverse media, weighing complex context and bias, on the two works and their critics' reception (Curio Class reception sheets). MA:Re9.1.III: build a rigorous critique of the student's own hybrid on their own, seek feedback on it from two readers, and record what changed. Words: hybrid, synthesize, polish, bias, context, rigorous. Showing: the hybrids in a class screening (two-wall where the room allows). End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MA:Cr3.1.IIIa, a guide look-for on the finished piece: forms joined into one meaning; MA:Cr3.1.IIIb, the kept revision log: every element polished for named viewers and setting (checked at the rough-cut test, the mid-unit checkpoint, and at the final); MA:Re8.1.III, a sitting item on the end check day: an unseen public domain piece's aims and impact, with a bias in its reception weighed; MA:Re9.1.III, the kept critique with its bias check and the two readers' feedback recorded. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the series (Level 4). Tools: the revision log, the bias check card, reception sheets, Curio Class's own. Free path: two paper strips shown side by side, a live score. Short path: 19 days | After mid-year because the hybrid uses unit 13's framework and toolset habits; before unit 15 because the cross-platform piece needs a hybrid made and judged first | Candidate: a third hybrid by a Native or Latin American maker, described and linked | Rapture shows men and women separated, in a setting named by the artist; the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page and the artist's own words first, and no student plays a character by an accent or dress. Atlantics concerns migrants lost at sea: the teacher previews, the unit shows only the parts it names, and no student is asked about their own family's migration. Hazards and controls: two projectors or screens with cords taped; headphones low; screen breaks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
317: | 15 | Level 3 | Across platforms, for real. *How do we make and promote a showing that changes something, and how do we know it did?* | 40 | ma-g11-present; ma-g11-sources | MA:Pr4.1.III; MA:Pr6.1.IIIa; MA:Pr6.1.IIIb; MA:Cn10.1.IIIa; MA:Cn10.1.IIIb | Producing (Pr4, Pr6); connecting (Cn10); creating in the cross-platform piece. Works: Du Bois's 1900 data portraits from unit 12, revisited as media that made new knowledge; Isuma's online platform (Igloolik, Nunavut, Canada, started 2008), Inuit-run media carried across film, television and the web in Inuit languages, described and linked to its own pages | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 2's channel plan and introspection with research. MA:Pr4.1.III: synthesize arts, media and school-subject content into a cohesive piece that holds across three platforms (the screen, a printed form and a live form), made by the student. MA:Pr6.1.IIIa: plan and promote a showing aimed at a stated, real effect in the school or community (a change asked of an audience), with a promotion plan. MA:Pr6.1.IIIb: judge and compare the results on their own (a before-and-after audience card, attendance, one response), fold in an improvement, and weigh the effects from personal to global. MA:Cn10.1.IIIa: seek out relevant, high-quality sources without being handed them, logged with a reason each is trusted. MA:Cn10.1.IIIb: explain and show how the piece produces new meaning, new knowledge or a cultural experience. Words: cross-platform, cohesive, promote, effect, result, new knowledge. Showing: the promoted showing in the school's space, with the printed and live forms. End-of-unit evidence (two check days: the showing and the results day), id by id: MA:Pr4.1.III, a look-for marked at the showing: one piece cohesive across three platforms; MA:Pr6.1.IIIa, the kept promotion plan: the stated effect, the audience and the promotion carried out; MA:Pr6.1.IIIb, a kept written results review on the second check day: results compared with the stated effect, an improvement folded in, effects weighed; MA:Cn10.1.IIIa, the kept source log at the mid-unit checkpoint: sources found alone, each judged relevant and high-quality with a reason; MA:Cn10.1.IIIb, a written account on the second check day: the new knowledge or cultural experience the piece produces. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the student-run series showing (Level 4). Tools: the promotion-plan sheet, the source log, the audience card, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed and live forms, a wall screen. Short path: 19 days | Last in Level 3 because the showing is built from the level's work and ends it before the year-end check in week 32; the purpose from unit 12 and the hybrid from unit 14 feed it | Candidate: the effect check written so it needs no data from students' accounts or devices | Isuma: the sensitivity agent reads Isuma's own pages and an Inuit organization's published words first; no student imitates Inuit language or practice. A real effect is asked only of audiences inside the school or community spaces the school allows; promotion uses the school's own boards and messages, never a student's accounts. Hazards and controls: a showing with the school's own adults; cords taped; no flashing light. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
"strand","grade","skill_id","standard","assumes","hands_on","model_in","model_out","words","cross_subject","notes"
"ma","9","ma-g9-ideas","MA:Cr1.1.I; MA:Cr2.1.I","ma-g8-ideas","ma-g10-ideas","storyboard","storyboard","idea method; goal; aesthetic standard; prototype; inspiration; setting","","Use named methods to form many ideas and set goals, then apply a short list of aesthetic standards to refine plans and prototypes for their setting. What changes: named methods and written aesthetic standards, where grade 8 chose methods freely. Assessed by a planning portfolio and prototype scored on a rubric for range, research and a stated aim. Level 1 bridge: the level's first unit (unit 1, before this row's unit 3) teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, as readiness, and claims no grade 8 id (at most 12 days in the full year, 6 on the 80-day short path, STANDARD.md 3.2)."
"ma","9","ma-g9-make","MA:Cr3.1.Ia; MA:Cr3.1.Ib","ma-g8-make","ma-g10-make","revision-log","revision-log","organize; style; emphasis; tone; aesthetic quality; personal goal","","Organize a piece's content and style with deliberate choices of emphasis or tone, and refine its look and sound to match the student's stated goals. What changes: choices are deliberate and tied to the student's own goals, where grade 8 blended conventions for set purposes. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a rubric for purpose, effect and technical quality. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
"ma","9","ma-g9-craft","MA:Pr5.1.Ia; MA:Pr5.1.Ib; MA:Pr5.1.Ic","ma-g8-craft","ma-g10-craft","production-roles","production-roles","choose a role; risk; constraint; combine; innovative; intent","","Choose and carry out a production role, take creative risks to meet a set limit or challenge, and combine usual and new tools to carry the intent. What changes: roles are chosen by the student and risk is taken on purpose, where grade 8 worked in assigned roles. Assessed by a production log and a tool-use task scored on a rubric for skill, risk and adapting. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
"ma","9","ma-g9-present","MA:Pr4.1.I; MA:Pr6.1.Ia; MA:Pr6.1.Ib","ma-g8-present","ma-g10-present","screening-plan","screening-plan","audience reaction; collection; format; judge; act; community","","Blend arts and media forms into one work planned for audience reaction, and design how a collection is shown and distributed for varied audiences. What changes: a collection, not a single piece, is designed for varied audiences. Assessed by a finished piece, a collection showing plan and a written review, scored on a rubric for message, format and improvement. Teacher note: students show work only in class or the school's own space, and a showing outside class needs each family's yes. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
"ma","9","ma-g9-read","MA:Re7.1.Ia; MA:Re7.1.Ib","ma-g8-read","ma-g10-read","look-describe-interpret","look-describe-interpret","preference; artist choice; sense; audience; intent; analyze","","Write an analysis of how parts, style and preferences show up in artists' choices, and how a piece steers experience through more than one sense. What changes: artists' choices are read and several senses are considered, where grade 8 contrasted styles. Assessed by a written analysis with evidence from the piece scored on a rubric for evidence and reasoning."
"ma","9","ma-g9-judge","MA:Re8.1.I; MA:Re9.1.I","ma-g8-judge","ma-g10-judge","critique-protocol","critique-protocol","received; cultural setting; key stage; identified criteria; aims; evaluate","","Critique how pieces are meant and received in personal and cultural settings, and review the student's own work at key production stages against named criteria. What changes: evaluation at key stages of production, not only on the finished piece. Assessed by a written critique at two production stages, scored on a rubric for criteria, evidence and the use of feedback. Teacher note: critique talks about the work, never the maker; students judge only media they have permission to share in class."
"ma","9","ma-g9-society","MA:Cn11.1.Ia; MA:Cn11.1.Ib","ma-g8-society","ma-g10-society","media-ethics-check","media-ethics-check","power; equality; trend; identity; digital identity; career","","Make a piece that shows how media ties to power, equality and identity, and weigh the legal, technical and career sides of media work, digital identity included. What changes: power, identity and digital identity enter the study, where grade 8 stopped at civic values. Assessed by a written or made investigation with a media-ethics check, scored on a rubric for evidence, ethics and a clear claim. Teacher note: Use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on. A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it. No student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
"ma","9","ma-g9-sources","MA:Cn10.1.Ia; MA:Cn10.1.Ib","ma-g8-sources","ma-g10-sources","source-log","source-log","outside source; own source; weave; original; online learning; widen","","Find, weigh and combine outside sources with the student's own ideas to shape an original piece, and show how media builds culture, for example through online learning. What changes: outside and own sources are woven into one original piece, where grade 8 used each separately. Assessed by a made piece with a source log and a written account, scored on a rubric for source quality, credit and meaning. Teacher note: use only public domain or openly licensed media, named and credited; fair use is explained, not relied on."
"ma","10","ma-g10-ideas","MA:Cr1.1.II; MA:Cr2.1.II","ma-g9-ideas","ma-g11-ideas","storyboard","storyboard","strategy; personal aesthetic; original; limits; prototype; approach","","Choose idea methods on purpose to sharpen goals and make an approach more original, then design a prototype in your own aesthetic within stated limits. What changes: methods are chosen strategically and the aesthetic is the student's own. Assessed by a planning portfolio and prototype that states the limits and the student's aesthetic, scored on a rubric for range, research and a stated aim. Level 2 opens with a review unit of at most 6 days that serves as placement."
"ma","10","ma-g10-make","MA:Cr3.1.IIa; MA:Cr3.1.IIb","ma-g9-make","ma-g11-make","revision-log","revision-log","continuity; juxtaposition; convention; look; technical; chosen audience","","Organize content and style with continuity or juxtaposition, and refine the look and technical parts so the work lands with chosen audiences. What changes: work is refined for the impact on a chosen audience, where level 1 honed quality toward personal goals. Assessed by a finished piece with a revision log, scored on a rubric for purpose, effect and technical quality. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
